#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "452ce4e4-8eb3-582b-acb2-99d10b1a08d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "452ce4e4-8eb3-582b-acb2-99d10b1a08d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.025" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556582} :answer-field/id #uuid "91adf0bc-26e8-5ef8-a01d-3f8b945f9b47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557149}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ad7acb8-7337-5a52-9975-3f2fd0fd9208" :question/math-academy-id "q-256827" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4{x}^{2}$ in the interval $[-2,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4{x}^{2})^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-8x)^{′}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n})+{x}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n})-8{x}_{n}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n})-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n})-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0})+{x}_{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0})-8{x}_{0}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0})-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0})-8} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)-8(2)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)-8} \\\\  & =0.505\\,007…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1})-8{x}_{1}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{1})-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{1})-8} \\\\  & =(0.505\\,007…)-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.505\\,007…)+(0.505\\,007…)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-8(0.505\\,007…)}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-(0.505\\,007…)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0.505\\,007…)-8} \\\\  & ≈0.025,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532251aa-a12e-5ee3-ac25-ec8eba7489a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.902" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "532251aa-a12e-5ee3-ac25-ec8eba7489a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.902" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556619} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d10b84c-ddb7-53d8-af30-74088c715ba6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c584116-fd97-52c4-9b38-c9c236f7fbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.879" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c584116-fd97-52c4-9b38-c9c236f7fbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.879" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556767} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b875e44-dea9-5add-bcd9-2d829e35888d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25247587-20e6-5dbb-bca0-455ff0ba3862" :question/math-academy-id "q-256858" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2}$ in the interval $[0.5,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-{x}^{2})^{′}=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-2x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-2x)^{′}=-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})-2{x}_{n})}{(-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-2)}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+{x}_{n}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{0})+{x}_{0}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{0})+1} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 1)+1}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 1)+1} \\\\  & =0.878\\,555…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{1})+{x}_{1}}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{1})+1} \\\\  & =(0.878\\,555…)-\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(0.878\\,555…))+0.878\\,555…}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(0.878\\,555…))+1} \\\\  & =0.901\\,873…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈0.879$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.902,$ rounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "636351f5-902f-5d1f-8fd5-b7b47c93ac44" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a17d347b-1b7e-543f-8583-c155340331ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.733" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a17d347b-1b7e-543f-8583-c155340331ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.733" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556824} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b467806-b4ba-5d03-bdb3-631ac4ebb45e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557145} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bc30e94-7338-5fe7-b7c9-f45fd26034d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.752" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bc30e94-7338-5fe7-b7c9-f45fd26034d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.752" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556523} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaa0b5de-3e08-5716-8224-20250363178e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557197}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbcfb522-f7cc-5bde-a522-5b2cd1adaa83" :question/math-academy-id "q-256862" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{3}$ in the interval $[3,4].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=3.6$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-{x}^{3})^{′}={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2})^{′}={e}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =3.6-\\frac{{e}^{3.6}-3(3.6)^{2}}{{e}^{3.6}-6(3.6)} \\\\  & =3.752\\,135…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(3.752\\,135…)-\\frac{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-3(3.752\\,135…)^{2}}{{e}^{3.752\\,135…}-6(3.752\\,135…)} \\\\  & =3.733\\,407…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈3.752$ and ${x}_{2}≈3.733,$ rounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557224 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57464dc7-bc2e-5b9c-aecb-331f510dce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556630} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac44f6e-0104-5745-83a5-1440a199990a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556679} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1f7571-d652-5d64-b073-b1826fb87653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556682} {:answer/id #uuid "a83a0cc8-6739-57e1-83af-4f3206bf0afd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556847} {:answer/id #uuid "c08ae5fa-bc80-5a70-8390-cd92ea33a585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d1f7571-d652-5d64-b073-b1826fb87653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556682} :answer-field/id #uuid "98ec7d6b-6a31-5769-9cf5-7523c10eb966" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2b6cfb99-55dc-52df-beff-19bcc94447aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-394147" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{6}-12x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{6}-12x)^{′}=6{x}^{5}-12, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{5}-12)^{′}=30{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{6{x}_{n}^{5}-12}{30{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(30{x}_{n}^{4})-(6{x}_{n}^{5}-12)}{30{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}_{n}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{n}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{24{x}_{0}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.5)^{5}+12}{30(1.5)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.279\\,012…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{24{x}_{1}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{1}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.279\\,012…)^{5}+12}{30(1.279\\,012…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.172\\,682…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{24{x}_{2}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.172\\,682…)^{5}+12}{30(1.172\\,682…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.149\\,659…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{24{x}_{3}^{5}+12}{30{x}_{3}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24(1.149\\,659…)^{5}+12}{30(1.149\\,659…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.148\\,699….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.15$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.15$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.15,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557251 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "de81bf51-3ea0-5c3b-b672-d760060cbe15" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function Using the Newton-Raphson Method for Optimization"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00608277-e348-507e-8515-26d9d8435c11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈2.91" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556426} {:answer/id #uuid "190d2d87-25cf-5280-bc2e-59163590ac8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈2.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556483} {:answer/id #uuid "95bbc949-7307-5b19-94e5-df2c13bf0a69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈3.05" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556790} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2ea8de-39f8-5969-9d65-3f5ca48f745d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈2.86" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556885} {:answer/id #uuid "c162ef2f-cc9b-56e9-8583-03470ec95cfa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈2.72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556895}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190d2d87-25cf-5280-bc2e-59163590ac8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x≈2.83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186556483} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed233c25-8953-5cf6-8918-dc5828016abb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186557200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f871b7b-ad2f-55da-b644-daab9cc00d69" :question/math-academy-id "q-394148" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5$ has a critical point in the interval $[2,4],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=3.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}+5)^{′}={e}^{x}-6x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6)-({e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n})}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=3.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}({x}_{0}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.5}(3.5-1)}{{e}^{3.5}-6} \\\\  & =3.053\\,190…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}({x}_{1}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}(3.053\\,190…-1)}{{e}^{3.053\\,190…}-6} \\\\  & =2.864\\,577…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}({x}_{2}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}(2.864\\,577…-1)}{{e}^{2.864\\,577…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,890…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{3}}({x}_{3}-1)}{{e}^{{x}_{3}}-6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}(2.833\\,890…-1)}{{e}^{2.833\\,890…}-6} \\\\  & =2.833\\,149….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈2.83$ and ${x}_{4}≈2.83$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈2.83,$ rounded to two decimal places."}]}
